<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
	The hold, the second half of an inspection.

	TienInspectWeapon.xml is the wind-up: the character brings the weapon up to look at it
	(vanilla's looting clip, the same for every weapon), and when that finishes the window
	opens and this node blends in over its m_BlendTime and takes over for as long
	as the player is reading. Two nodes rather than one because the two halves are doing
	different things - one is an action with a duration, the other is a pose being held -
	and because a single clip stretched across both reads as a loop nobody chose.

	The clip is the mod's own, Bob_TienInspect_1H_Hold (built by scripts/anim/clips/inspect.py):
	an 8 second loop of looking the weapon over - turn it to one side and pause, to the other
	and pause, then along the edge, and back - with the head following it. This is the
	one-handed clip and the fallback; the _2H, _Handgun and _Rifle variants
	take over by the Weapon variable.

	The hold can run for the whole sandbox ceiling, so it has to survive looping: each hold
	clip's last frame leads back into its first by less than an ordinary frame's step.

	m_Looped is true here and false everywhere in vanilla's actions folder, because every
	vanilla action is short enough to finish. This one is not, and the game's own idle, walk
	and run nodes are the precedent for a node that has to keep going.

	There is no clip for lowering the weapon: every exit (window closed, another action, a run)
	clears PerformingAction and the engine cross-fades this node out towards whatever matches
	next, bone by bone. The pose does not split the weight between the two nodes:
	AnimationPlayer lets the heavier track take its weight first and the other only fills what
	is left, so the incoming idle's own blend-in (0.2 s) decides the fade, and a longer
	m_BlendOutTime here would only hold the pose a frame longer and then snap harder. The
	m_Transitions below give both sides 0.5 s instead (AdvancedAnimator finds a transition by
	the target node's name, and LiveAnimNode.getBlendInTime then uses its m_blendInTime for the
	idle), so the weapon comes down over half a second - the way vanilla's rack-while-aiming
	actions hand over to IdleRifle. Every idle node is listed, sneaking ones included; exits to
	anything else (walking, another action) keep that node's own blend-in. The _2H, _Handgun
	and _Rifle holds carry the same list.

	No m_SubStateBoneWeights, for the same reason TienInspectWeapon.xml has none - see the
	comment there.

	Persistent mode (InspectMode 2) plays this node too, as the whole of its one timed action
	(ISTienInspectWeaponPersistentAction): the studying pose for the raise time, then the
	window opens and the character is free again.
-->
<animNode>
	<m_Name>TienInspectWeaponHold</m_Name>
	<m_AnimName>Bob_TienInspect_1H_Hold</m_AnimName>
	<m_Looped>true</m_Looped>
	<m_BlendTime>0.30</m_BlendTime>
	<m_SpeedScale>1.0</m_SpeedScale>
	<m_SyncTrackingEnabled>false</m_SyncTrackingEnabled>
	<m_Conditions x_name="3e59b7c4-8a12-4f6d-b0e7-2c9d41f8a6b3">
		<m_Name>PerformingAction</m_Name>
		<m_Type>STRING</m_Type>
		<m_Value>TienInspectWeaponHold</m_Value>
	</m_Conditions>
	<m_Transitions>
		<m_Target>Idle</m_Target>
		<m_blendInTime>0.5</m_blendInTime>
		<m_blendOutTime>0.5</m_blendOutTime>
	</m_Transitions>
	<m_Transitions>
		<m_Target>Idle2Handed</m_Target>
		<m_blendInTime>0.5</m_blendInTime>
		<m_blendOutTime>0.5</m_blendOutTime>
	</m_Transitions>
	<m_Transitions>
		<m_Target>IdleChainsaw</m_Target>
		<m_blendInTime>0.5</m_blendInTime>
		<m_blendOutTime>0.5</m_blendOutTime>
	</m_Transitions>
	<m_Transitions>
		<m_Target>IdleHandgun</m_Target>
		<m_blendInTime>0.5</m_blendInTime>
		<m_blendOutTime>0.5</m_blendOutTime>
	</m_Transitions>
	<m_Transitions>
		<m_Target>IdleHeavy</m_Target>
		<m_blendInTime>0.5</m_blendInTime>
		<m_blendOutTime>0.5</m_blendOutTime>
	</m_Transitions>
	<m_Transitions>
		<m_Target>IdleRifle</m_Target>
		<m_blendInTime>0.5</m_blendInTime>
		<m_blendOutTime>0.5</m_blendOutTime>
	</m_Transitions>
